(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "8.4", title: "Representing text, images and sound", q: "How does everything on a screen or in a speaker become binary?", lessons: [
{ t: "Binary recap and retrieval quiz", o: "I can convert between binary and denary and add binary numbers.",
  k: ["binary: the base 2 number system, using 0 and 1", "byte: 8 bits", "overflow: when a result needs more bits than are available"],
  d: ["Write the eight place values of a byte.", "128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1."],
  s: ["Conversions|Binary to denary: add the place values that have a 1|Denary to binary: subtract the largest place value that fits|Always write 8 bits",
      "Addition|0+0=0, 0+1=1, 1+1=0 carry 1, 1+1+1=1 carry 1|A carry out of the left-hand column is overflow|Check in denary",
      "Bits and values|n bits give 2 to the power n patterns|8 bits: 256 patterns, values 0 to 255|Units: bit, nibble, byte, KiB, MiB"],
  a: "Complete the 20-question retrieval quiz on Year 7 binary, mark it, then use the Data page of CS Toolkit to practise the question type you found hardest. Support: use the place-value strip. Stretch: add three bytes together and explain how carries of more than 1 are handled.",
  w: [["Convert 01011010 to denary.", "90."], ["Convert 173 to binary.", "10101101."], ["Add 01001110 + 00101011.", "01111001 (78 + 43 = 121)."], ["How many values can 6 bits represent?", "64."], ["Explain what overflow is, with an example.", "The result is too big for the bits available. For example 200 + 100 = 300, which does not fit in 8 bits (maximum 255)."]],
  x: ["Which question type will you practise first?", "A named type."] },
{ t: "Hexadecimal: why and how", o: "I can convert between binary, denary and hexadecimal.",
  k: ["hexadecimal: the base 16 number system, using 0 to 9 and A to F", "nibble: 4 bits, which make one hexadecimal digit", "base 16: each place is worth 16 times the one to its right"],
  d: ["Read this aloud to a partner and ask them to write it down: 1011010011110001. How did it go?", "It is slow and easy to get wrong. Long binary is hard for people."],
  s: ["Why hexadecimal?|Binary is long and easy to misread|One hex digit stands for exactly 4 bits|It is a shorthand for people: the computer still uses binary",
      "The digits|0 to 9, then A = 10, B = 11, C = 12, D = 13, E = 14, F = 15|Place values: 16 and 1|9D = 9 x 16 + 13 = 157",
      "Binary to hex|Split the byte into two nibbles|Convert each nibble to one hex digit|1010 1100 becomes A C, so AC"],
  a: "Complete the hex digit table from 0 to F, then convert ten bytes to hex, ten hex values to binary and five to denary. Check with the Data page of CS Toolkit. Support: use the nibble lookup table. Stretch: convert the four-digit hex value 1F4 to denary.",
  w: [["What is the denary value of the hex digit C?", "12."], ["Convert the binary 1010 1100 to hexadecimal.", "AC."], ["Convert hex 3E to binary.", "0011 1110."], ["Convert hex 2F to denary.", "47 (2 x 16 + 15)."], ["Explain why programmers use hexadecimal instead of binary.", "It is shorter and easier to read and write, so fewer mistakes are made, and each hex digit converts directly to 4 bits."]],
  x: ["What is the largest two-digit hex number, and its denary value?", "FF, which is 255."] },
{ t: "Lab: hex colour codes", o: "I can use hexadecimal to describe colours.",
  k: ["RGB: a way of making colours by mixing red, green and blue light", "colour code: a six-digit hex number that describes a colour", "intensity: how bright a colour channel is, from 0 to 255"],
  d: ["On a screen, which three colours of light are mixed to make every other colour?", "Red, green and blue."],
  s: ["RGB|Each pixel has a red, a green and a blue value|Each value goes from 0 (off) to 255 (full)|255 in hex is FF",
      "Colour codes|Six hex digits: two each for red, green and blue|`#FF0000` is red, `#00FF00` is green, `#0000FF` is blue|`#000000` is black, `#FFFFFF` is white",
      "Mixing|`#FFFF00` is red plus green: yellow|Equal values give grey: `#808080`|You used these in CSS last unit"],
  a: "Use a colour picker to find the hex codes for ten colours, predict the colour of eight codes before checking, and create a three-colour palette for a website with the codes recorded. Support: match the codes to colour swatches. Stretch: convert `#3C82F0` into three denary values and describe the colour before you check.",
  w: [["What does RGB stand for?", "Red, green, blue."], ["What colour is `#00FF00`?", "Green."], ["What colour is `#FFFFFF`?", "White."], ["Write the hex code for yellow (full red and full green, no blue).", "#FFFF00"], ["Explain why a hex colour code has six digits.", "Two digits (one byte) for each of red, green and blue."]],
  x: ["How many bits does one hex colour code represent?", "24."] },
{ t: "Characters: ASCII", o: "I can explain how text is stored using character codes.",
  k: ["character set: the list of characters a computer can represent, each with its own code", "ASCII: a character set that uses 7 bits, giving 128 characters", "character code: the number that stands for a character"],
  d: ["A computer can only store numbers. How could it store the letter A?", "Give every character a number and store the number."],
  s: ["Character codes|Each character has a number: its character code|Pressing a key sends the code, not the shape of the letter|The code is stored in binary",
      "ASCII|7 bits, so 128 characters|A is 65, B is 66; a is 97; the digit 0 is 48|Capital and small letters have different codes",
      "Patterns to know|Letters are in alphabetical order, so C is 67|A small letter is 32 more than its capital|The character '5' is not the number 5"],
  a: "Use the ASCII table to encode your name in denary and binary and decode the hidden message. Then answer the pattern questions without the table. Check your codes with the Data page of CS Toolkit. Support: encode a three-letter word. Stretch: explain how a program could change a capital letter to a small letter using only arithmetic.",
  w: [["What is a character set?", "The list of characters a computer can represent, each with its own code."], ["How many characters can 7-bit ASCII represent?", "128."], ["A is 65. What is the code for D?", "68."], ["A is 65 and a is 97. What is the code for small letter c?", "99."], ["Explain why the character '7' and the number 7 are stored differently.", "The character '7' is stored as its character code (55). The number 7 is stored as the binary value 7."]],
  x: ["Write the word CAB as three ASCII codes.", "67, 65, 66."] },
{ t: "Unicode and the world's writing systems", o: "I can explain why Unicode is needed.",
  k: ["Unicode: a character set that includes the characters of the world's languages", "emoji: a small picture character that has its own Unicode code", "code point: the number given to a character in Unicode"],
  d: ["ASCII has 128 characters. Can it store Chinese, Arabic, Russian or Vietnamese text? Why?", "No. There are far more characters in those languages than 128 codes."],
  s: ["The limit of ASCII|128 characters: enough for English letters, digits and punctuation|No accents, no other alphabets, no emoji|Different countries once used different codes for the same number",
      "Unicode|One character set for every language|More than 100 000 characters|Uses more bits per character: 16 or more",
      "Trade-off|More characters need more bits|So the same text can take more storage|The first 128 Unicode codes match ASCII, so old text still works"],
  a: "Find the Unicode code points for characters from three writing systems, including your own language if it is not English, and for two emoji. Then complete the ASCII and Unicode comparison table. Support: use the provided code chart. Stretch: calculate how many characters 16 bits can represent and explain why even that is not enough for everything.",
  w: [["What is Unicode?", "A character set that includes the characters of the world's languages."], ["Give one thing Unicode can represent that ASCII cannot.", "Any one: accented letters, other alphabets, Chinese characters, emoji."], ["Why does Unicode need more bits per character than ASCII?", "There are many more characters, so more different codes are needed."], ["How many different characters can 16 bits represent?", "65 536."], ["Explain one disadvantage of Unicode compared with ASCII.", "Each character can need more bits, so text files can be larger."]],
  x: ["Why do old ASCII files still display correctly today?", "The first 128 Unicode codes are the same as ASCII."] },
{ t: "Lab: secret messages in binary and hex", o: "I can encode and decode text using character codes.",
  k: ["encode: to turn information into code", "decode: to turn code back into information", "hex dump: a display of data as hexadecimal values"],
  d: ["Decode these ASCII codes: 72, 105.", "Hi."],
  s: ["Encoding|Look up each character's code|Write it in binary (8 bits) or as two hex digits|H is 72, which is 01001000 or 48",
      "Decoding|Split the bits into bytes|Convert each byte to denary|Look up the character",
      "Why hex is handy|`48 69` is much shorter than `01001000 01101001`|Programmers read files this way|Fewer digits means fewer mistakes"],
  a: "Write a short message, encode it in hex and pass it to a partner to decode. Then decode the three challenge messages (one in binary, one in hex, one mixed). Support: decode the hex message with the lookup table. Stretch: the last challenge has had 1 added to every code. Work out the rule and decode it.",
  w: [["Encode the letter H (72) as 8-bit binary.", "01001000."], ["Encode the letter H (72) in hex.", "48."], ["Decode the hex codes 43 41 54.", "CAT (67, 65, 84)."], ["How many hex digits are needed for a five-letter word?", "10."], ["Explain why the same message is shorter in hex than in binary.", "Each hex digit stands for 4 bits, so only a quarter as many digits are needed."]],
  x: ["Decode: 48 69.", "Hi."] },
{ t: "Bitmap images: pixels and resolution", o: "I can explain how a bitmap image is stored.",
  k: ["pixel: the smallest dot of an image, with one colour", "bitmap: an image stored as a grid of pixels", "resolution: the number of pixels in an image, given as width by height"],
  d: ["What happens when you zoom a long way into a photo on a screen?", "You see small squares: the pixels."],
  s: ["Pixels|An image is a grid of tiny squares|Each pixel has one colour|The colour is stored as a binary number",
      "Resolution|Width x height in pixels, for example 1920 x 1080|More pixels show more detail|More pixels also mean a larger file",
      "A black and white image|1 bit per pixel: 1 for black, 0 for white|An 8 x 8 image needs 64 bits|The computer stores the bits row by row"],
  a: "Decode the 8 x 8 grid of bits into a picture, then design your own 8 x 8 image and write it as eight bytes. Compare the same picture drawn at 8 x 8 and 16 x 16. Support: colour in the grid one row at a time. Stretch: write each row of your image as two hex digits.",
  w: [["What is a pixel?", "The smallest dot of an image, with one colour."], ["What is the resolution of an image?", "The number of pixels, given as width by height."], ["An image is 10 pixels wide and 8 pixels high. How many pixels is that?", "80."], ["How many bits are needed for an 8 x 8 black and white image?", "64 bits (8 bytes)."], ["Explain the effect of increasing the resolution of an image.", "It shows more detail and looks sharper, but the file is larger because there are more pixels to store."]],
  x: ["Draw the row 00111100 as pixels (1 is black).", "Two white, four black, two white."] },
{ t: "Colour depth", o: "I can explain colour depth and how it affects an image.",
  k: ["colour depth: the number of bits used to store the colour of each pixel", "palette: the set of colours available in an image", "true colour: 24-bit colour, giving over 16 million colours"],
  d: ["With 1 bit per pixel you can have 2 colours. How many with 2 bits per pixel?", "4."],
  s: ["More bits, more colours|1 bit: 2 colours. 2 bits: 4. 3 bits: 8|8 bits: 256 colours|The number of colours is 2 to the power of the colour depth",
      "True colour|24 bits per pixel: 8 each for red, green and blue|16 777 216 colours|More than the eye can easily tell apart",
      "The cost|Every pixel needs more bits|Doubling the colour depth doubles the size of the pixel data|Low colour depth makes photos look blocky or banded"],
  a: "Complete the table of colour depth and number of colours from 1 to 8 bits. Then colour the 2-bit image using its four-colour palette and compare the three versions of the photo at different colour depths. Support: complete the table to 4 bits. Stretch: how many bits are needed for exactly 100 colours, and how many colours are wasted?",
  w: [["What is colour depth?", "The number of bits used to store the colour of each pixel."], ["How many colours can a 3-bit colour depth give?", "8."], ["How many bits per pixel does true colour use?", "24."], ["An image needs 16 different colours. What colour depth is required?", "4 bits."], ["Explain the effect of reducing the colour depth of a photograph.", "Fewer colours are available, so the image looks less realistic, but the file is smaller."]],
  x: ["How many colours does an 8-bit colour depth give?", "256."] },
{ t: "Lab: pixel art, encode and decode", o: "I can encode an image by hand and decode someone else's.",
  k: ["encode: to turn an image into numbers", "metadata: extra data about the image, such as its width, height and colour depth", "decode: to turn the numbers back into an image"],
  d: ["If someone sends you 64 bits and says 'it's a picture', what else do you need to know to draw it?", "The width and height, and how many bits each pixel uses."],
  s: ["Design|A 10 x 10 picture with four colours|Choose a 2-bit code for each colour: 00, 01, 10, 11|Write the palette down",
      "Encode|Write the code for every pixel, row by row|Record the metadata: width, height, colour depth|Without it the bits cannot be turned back into the image",
      "Decode|Swap sheets with a partner|Use their metadata and palette|Colour the grid and compare with the original"],
  a: "Design, encode and swap a 10 x 10 four-colour image, including its metadata and palette. Decode your partner's and check it against their original. Support: use an 8 x 8 grid with two colours. Stretch: calculate the total number of bits for your image and for the same image in 24-bit colour.",
  w: [["What is metadata?", "Extra data about the image, such as its width, height and colour depth."], ["How many bits per pixel are needed for four colours?", "2."], ["How many bits store a 10 x 10 image at 2 bits per pixel?", "200."], ["What would happen if the receiver used the wrong width?", "The rows would break in the wrong places and the picture would be scrambled."], ["Explain why the palette must be sent with the image.", "The codes only say which colour number each pixel is. The palette says which actual colour each number means."]],
  x: ["Did your partner's decoded image match your original? If not, where was the error?", "A comparison."] },
{ t: "Image file size calculations", o: "I can calculate the file size of a bitmap image.",
  k: ["file size: the amount of storage a file needs", "formula: a rule for calculating", "kibibyte (KiB): 1024 bytes"],
  d: ["An image has 100 pixels and each pixel uses 8 bits. How many bits altogether?", "800."],
  s: ["The formula|File size in bits = width x height x colour depth|Divide by 8 for bytes|Divide by 1024 for KiB, and again for MiB",
      "Worked example|100 x 100 pixels, 8-bit colour|100 x 100 x 8 = 80 000 bits|80 000 / 8 = 10 000 bytes, about 9.8 KiB",
      "What changes the size|Double the width and the height: four times the size|Double the colour depth: twice the size|Real files are a little larger because of metadata"],
  a: "Calculate the file sizes of eight images, showing every step and giving the unit. Then answer the 'what if' questions about changing resolution and colour depth. Support: use the calculation frame. Stretch: a camera takes 4000 x 3000 photos in 24-bit colour. How many uncompressed photos fit on a 32 GiB card?",
  w: [["Write the formula for image file size in bits.", "Width x height x colour depth."], ["An image is 8 x 8 pixels with 1-bit colour. Give its size in bytes.", "64 bits = 8 bytes."], ["An image is 100 x 100 pixels with 8-bit colour. Give its size in bytes.", "80 000 bits = 10 000 bytes."], ["An image is 640 x 480 pixels with 24-bit colour. Give its size in KiB.", "7 372 800 bits = 921 600 bytes = 900 KiB."], ["An image's width and height are both doubled. Explain what happens to the file size.", "It becomes four times larger, because there are four times as many pixels."]],
  x: ["What two things can you reduce to make an image file smaller?", "Resolution and colour depth."] },
{ t: "Sound: sampling", o: "I can explain how sound is stored digitally.",
  k: ["sample: one measurement of the height of a sound wave", "sample rate: the number of samples taken each second, measured in hertz", "sample resolution: the number of bits used to store each sample"],
  d: ["Sound is a smooth, continuous wave. A computer can only store numbers. What could it do?", "Measure the wave many times a second and store each measurement."],
  s: ["Sampling|Sound is a continuous (analogue) wave|The computer measures its height at regular intervals|Each measurement is a sample, stored in binary",
      "Quality|Higher sample rate: more samples each second, closer to the real wave|Higher sample resolution: each sample is measured more precisely|CD quality: 44 100 samples a second at 16 bits",
      "File size|Size in bits = sample rate x sample resolution x seconds|8000 Hz, 8 bits, 10 seconds = 640 000 bits = 80 000 bytes|Better quality means a bigger file"],
  a: "On the printed sound wave, take samples at the marked intervals and record them, then redraw the wave from your samples at a low and a high sample rate and compare. Calculate the size of four recordings. Support: sample at the low rate only. Stretch: explain why telephone audio uses a much lower sample rate than music.",
  w: [["What is a sample?", "One measurement of the height of a sound wave."], ["What is the sample rate?", "The number of samples taken each second."], ["What is the sample resolution?", "The number of bits used to store each sample."], ["Calculate the size in bytes of a 10-second recording at 8000 Hz with 8-bit samples.", "8000 x 8 x 10 = 640 000 bits = 80 000 bytes."], ["Explain the effect of doubling the sample rate.", "The recording is closer to the original sound, so quality improves, but the file size doubles."]],
  x: ["Name the two settings that control the quality of a sound recording.", "Sample rate and sample resolution."] },
{ t: "Lab: record audio at different settings", o: "I can investigate how recording settings affect quality and file size.",
  k: ["waveform: a picture of a sound wave on screen", "trade-off: gaining one thing by giving up another", "fair test: changing one thing at a time"],
  d: ["If you halve the sample rate of a recording, what happens to its file size?", "It halves."],
  s: ["The investigation|Record the same 10-second phrase|Save it at three sample rates|Then at two sample resolutions",
      "Record your results|Setting, file size, and a quality score out of 5|Zoom in on the waveform to see the samples|Change one setting at a time: a fair test",
      "Look for the pattern|Does file size double when the sample rate doubles?|At which setting can you first hear a difference?|What is the smallest file that still sounds acceptable?"],
  a: "Using the audio editor, record a 10-second phrase and export it at the settings in the table. Record file size and quality for each, then write three conclusions. Support: use the three pre-recorded files. Stretch: compare your measured file sizes with the calculated sizes and explain any difference.",
  w: [["Which setting gave the largest file?", "The highest sample rate and resolution."], ["What happened to the file size when the sample rate doubled?", "It doubled (approximately)."], ["At which setting did the sound first become noticeably worse?", "A setting from the student's results."], ["Why change only one setting at a time?", "So you know which setting caused the change: a fair test."], ["Explain the trade-off between quality and file size for sound.", "Higher sample rate and resolution give better quality but need more storage and take longer to send."]],
  x: ["Which setting would you choose for a voice message, and why?", "A low or medium setting, because speech stays clear and the file is small."] },
{ t: "Why compress? Lossy and lossless", o: "I can explain why files are compressed and compare lossy with lossless compression.",
  k: ["compression: reducing the size of a file", "lossless: compression that lets the original file be rebuilt exactly", "lossy: compression that permanently removes some data"],
  d: ["A film in full quality would be hundreds of gibibytes. How can you stream it on a phone?", "It is compressed to a much smaller size."],
  s: ["Why compress?|Smaller files use less storage|They are quicker to send and download|Streaming uses less data",
      "Lossless|No data is lost: the original can be rebuilt exactly|Essential for text, programs and spreadsheets|Examples: zip files, PNG images",
      "Lossy|Removes detail people are unlikely to notice|Much smaller files, but the removed data is gone for good|Examples: JPEG photos, MP3 music, streamed video"],
  a: "Decide whether lossy or lossless compression is suitable for each of the twelve files and justify your choice. Then compare the three versions of the same photo saved at different quality settings. Support: sort the files into 'must be exact' and 'can lose a little'. Stretch: explain why saving a lossy file again and again makes it worse each time.",
  w: [["What is compression?", "Reducing the size of a file."], ["Give two reasons for compressing a file.", "Any two: less storage, faster to send, uses less data when streaming."], ["What is the difference between lossy and lossless compression?", "Lossless allows the original to be rebuilt exactly. Lossy permanently removes some data."], ["Which type must be used for a text document, and why?", "Lossless, because every character matters and none can be lost."], ["Explain why lossy compression is acceptable for a holiday photo but not for a computer program.", "A photo still looks right with some detail removed. A program with any data missing would not run."]],
  x: ["Give one example each of a lossy and a lossless file type.", "For example JPEG or MP3 (lossy); zip or PNG (lossless)."] },
{ t: "Run-length encoding", o: "I can compress data using run-length encoding.",
  k: ["run-length encoding (RLE): a lossless method that stores repeated values as a count and the value", "run: a sequence of identical values next to each other", "redundancy: data that is repeated and so can be stored more briefly"],
  d: ["How could you shorten 'WWWWWWWWBB' when reading it out to someone?", "Say 'eight W, two B'."],
  s: ["The idea|Look for runs of the same value|Store how many, then the value|WWWWBBWWWWWW becomes 4W 2B 6W",
      "Decoding|Read each pair|Write the value out that many times|The original is rebuilt exactly, so it is lossless",
      "When it works|Well: images with large areas of one colour, such as logos|Badly: photos, where neighbouring pixels differ|BWBWBWBW becomes 1B 1W 1B 1W... which is longer"],
  a: "Compress the ten rows of pixels with RLE and decompress five encoded rows. Count the characters before and after to find how much was saved. Support: compress rows that use two colours only. Stretch: find a row for which RLE doubles the size, and explain why.",
  w: [["What does RLE stand for?", "Run-length encoding."], ["Compress WWWWBBWWWWWW with RLE.", "4W 2B 6W."], ["Decompress 3R 5G 2R.", "RRRGGGGGRR."], ["Is RLE lossy or lossless? Explain.", "Lossless, because the original data can be rebuilt exactly."], ["Explain why RLE works badly on a photograph.", "Neighbouring pixels are rarely identical, so there are few long runs, and storing a count for each pixel makes the file larger."]],
  x: ["Which image would RLE compress better: a flag or a photo of a forest?", "A flag."] },
{ t: "Lab: compress pixel art with RLE", o: "I can measure how well RLE compresses different images.",
  k: ["compression ratio: the original size divided by the compressed size", "efficient: achieving a result with little waste", "investigate: to test ideas and look for patterns"],
  d: ["An image is 100 characters before RLE and 25 after. What is the compression ratio?", "4 to 1."],
  s: ["Method|Take a pixel art image as rows of colour letters|Compress each row with RLE|Count the symbols before and after",
      "Compare|A simple logo with large blocks of colour|A detailed image with many small changes|A chequerboard",
      "Conclude|Which compressed best?|Did any get bigger?|What kind of image suits RLE?"],
  a: "Compress the three images row by row, record the sizes before and after, and calculate each compression ratio. Then design a 10 x 10 image that compresses as much as possible and one that compresses as badly as possible. Support: compress the logo only. Stretch: suggest a change to RLE that would help the chequerboard.",
  w: [["What is a compression ratio?", "The original size divided by the compressed size."], ["Which image had the best compression ratio?", "The logo, with large blocks of colour."], ["Which image became larger after RLE?", "The chequerboard."], ["A file is 800 bytes before and 200 bytes after compression. Give the ratio.", "4 to 1."], ["Explain what your investigation shows about when to use RLE.", "It suits data with long runs of repeated values and should be avoided when values change often."]],
  x: ["Describe your best-compressing design in a few words.", "For example one solid colour, or wide horizontal stripes."] },
{ t: "Revision: mixed calculations", o: "I can answer mixed questions on text, images, sound and compression.",
  k: ["revise: to go over learning again", "working: the steps that show how an answer was reached", "unit: what a number is measured in, such as bytes"],
  d: ["Write the two file size formulas from this unit.", "Image: width x height x colour depth. Sound: sample rate x sample resolution x seconds."],
  s: ["Numbers and text|Hex: one digit per nibble, A to F for 10 to 15|ASCII: 7 bits, 128 characters; Unicode: every language, more bits|Character codes are in alphabetical order",
      "Images and sound|Resolution and colour depth set image quality and size|Sample rate and sample resolution set sound quality and size|Always show the formula, the working and the unit",
      "Compression|Lossless: nothing lost, original rebuilt exactly|Lossy: detail removed for a much smaller file|RLE: count and value for each run"],
  a: "Complete the mixed revision paper in pairs, mark it with the answer sheet and list the two question types you need to practise. Support: use the formula card. Stretch: write a five-mark question with a full mark scheme.",
  w: [["Convert hex B7 to denary.", "183 (11 x 16 + 7)."], ["How many colours does a 4-bit colour depth give?", "16."], ["Calculate the size in bytes of a 50 x 40 image with 8-bit colour.", "50 x 40 x 8 = 16 000 bits = 2000 bytes."], ["Compress AAAABBBCCCCCC with RLE.", "4A 3B 6C."], ["Explain why music streaming services use lossy compression.", "Files are much smaller, so they stream quickly and use less data, and most listeners do not notice the detail removed."]],
  x: ["Which two question types will you practise?", "Two named types."] },
{ t: "Assessed test", o: "I can show what I know about how data is represented.",
  k: ["test conditions: working alone, in silence, without notes", "show your working: write the steps, not only the answer", "justify: to give reasons for a choice"],
  d: ["What three things should every calculation answer include?", "The formula or method, the working, and the unit."],
  s: ["The test|Conversions between binary, denary and hex|One image and one sound calculation|A compare-and-justify question on compression",
      "Gaining marks|Working earns marks even if the final answer is wrong|Give units|For 'justify', link your reason to the situation",
      "Checking|Convert back to check conversions|Estimate: is the size sensible?|Reread the question"],
  a: "Complete the written test under test conditions. Support: the two file size formulas are provided. Stretch: attempt the extension question on why vector graphics do not lose quality when enlarged.",
  w: [["Convert the binary 1100 1000 to hex.", "C8."], ["Why is Unicode used instead of ASCII for a website read around the world?", "It can represent the characters of every language, and ASCII cannot."], ["Calculate the size in bytes of a 200 x 100 image with 4-bit colour.", "200 x 100 x 4 = 80 000 bits = 10 000 bytes."], ["Calculate the size in KiB of 16 seconds of sound at 8000 Hz with 8-bit samples.", "8000 x 8 x 16 = 1 024 000 bits = 128 000 bytes = 125 KiB."], ["A company logo must be stored exactly. Recommend lossy or lossless compression and justify it.", "Lossless, because the logo must look identical every time, and logos have large areas of one colour that compress well without loss."]],
  x: ["Which question were you least sure about?", "A question number."] },
{ t: "Feedback, corrections and reflection", o: "I can correct my mistakes and reflect on my learning.",
  k: ["correction: a rewritten answer that fixes a mistake", "misconception: a wrong idea that causes mistakes", "target: a specific thing to work on next"],
  d: ["Look at the first question you lost marks on. Was it a slip, a gap or a misreading?", "A labelled mistake."],
  s: ["Types of mistake|Slip: you knew the method|Gap: you did not know the method|Misreading: you answered a different question",
      "Corrections|Redo each wrong question in full, with working|Check calculations with the Data page of CS Toolkit|Write what went wrong in a few words",
      "Reflect|What can I do now that I could not before?|Which idea links to something outside school?|What is my target?"],
  a: "Complete corrections for every question you lost marks on, labelling each mistake type, then complete the unit reflection. Support: your teacher has chosen three questions to correct. Stretch: explain to a partner how a photo on your phone is stored, using six key words.",
  w: [["Copy one question you got wrong and correct it in full.", "A fully corrected answer."], ["Was it a slip, a gap or a misreading?", "A labelled mistake type."], ["Name one thing you can do now that you could not six weeks ago.", "A unit skill."], ["Give one example from everyday life where compression is used.", "For example streaming music or video, sending photos, zip files."], ["Set one target and say how you will practise it.", "A specific target and plan."]],
